- Diseases during pregnancy: Presence of chronic or non-chronic diseases may increase stress and risk of postpartum depression.
- Addiction: Addiction (smoking, drinking, drugs) may correlate with higher depression risk.
- Current monthly income: Lower income or no income may increase stress and depression risk; higher income may be protective.
- Trust and share feelings: Being able to trust and share feelings is protective; inability may increase depression risk.
- Residence: City or village may have different stressors; city living might increase stress, but village might have less access to care.
- Regular checkups: Regular medical checkups may reduce depression risk through better health monitoring and support.
- Breastfeed: Breastfeeding may be protective through hormonal and bonding effects; not breastfeeding may increase risk.
- Newborn illness: Newborn illness adds stress and may increase risk of postpartum depression.

